  "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ {{2(p-4)}_{\u23df}}_{a}x & ={{3}_{\u23df}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
